<h2>Where Section 31 sits in the scheme</h2>
<p>A protection order under Section 18 of the DV Act prohibits the respondent from committing acts of domestic violence, aiding or abetting them, entering the aggrieved person&rsquo;s workplace or school, attempting to communicate with her, alienating joint assets or operating joint lockers, and causing violence to her relatives. The order may be interim under Section 23 or final after inquiry. Until breach, the proceedings carry no criminal consequence for the respondent. Section 31 changes that on the first violation: the breach is a fresh, self-contained offence, independent of whether the original conduct complained of was ever prosecuted.</p>

<h2>The mechanics of a breach prosecution</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Report the breach.</strong> The aggrieved person may complain to the Protection Officer, to the police, or directly to the Magistrate. Because the offence is cognizable, the police can register an FIR on the complaint. A contemporaneous record — DD entry, written complaint with date and time, medical record if violence occurred — anchors the prosecution.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Investigation and cognizance.</strong> The police investigate as in any cognizable case. Prosecution proceeds before the Magistrate, and under Section 31(2) the matter goes, so far as practicable, to the very Magistrate whose order was breached — a court already familiar with the history.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Charges, including add-ons.</strong> Section 31(3) permits the Magistrate, at the charge stage, to frame charges under Section 498A IPC or other IPC provisions, or the Dowry Prohibition Act, 1961, if the facts disclose those offences — consolidating what would otherwise be parallel prosecutions.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Trial.</strong> The breach must be proved beyond reasonable doubt, but Section 32(2) expressly permits conviction on the sole testimony of the aggrieved person.</div>
</div>

<h2>What each side should understand</h2>
<table class="law">
  <tr><th>For the aggrieved person</th><th>For the respondent</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Precision in the protection order pays off later: an order that names the prohibited acts, places and modes of contact makes breach easy to establish.</td><td>Read the order as a strict boundary. &ldquo;Innocent&rdquo; contact — messages seeking reconciliation, appearing at the school, calls through relatives — can constitute breach if the order prohibits communication.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Document every incident immediately; delay invites the defence of fabrication.</td><td>If circumstances genuinely require interaction (shared children, joint property), seek modification of the order under Section 25 rather than self-help.</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Press for Section 31(3) consolidation where fresh cruelty accompanies the breach.</td><td>Remember the offence is non-bailable; an arrest is a realistic consequence, and anticipatory protection may need to be considered on proper advice.</td></tr>
</table>

<h2>Breach of other orders: the enforcement map</h2>
<p>Section 31 is confined to protection orders. Monetary relief that goes unpaid is enforced under Section 20(6) — including by directing an employer or debtor of the respondent to pay the aggrieved person directly — and through the mechanism the Magistrate adopts for maintenance enforcement. Residence orders are implemented with the assistance of Protection Officers and, where the order so directs, the police under Section 19(5) and (7). A respondent&rsquo;s conduct will often engage more than one of these tracks at once, and the choice of enforcement route is a matter of strategy: the criminal route under Section 31 carries the greatest deterrent force, while the monetary mechanisms recover arrears more directly.</p>
<div class="note">The DV Act proceedings themselves are now conducted under the procedural framework of the BNSS, 2023, which replaced the CrPC on 1 July 2024; references to Section 498A IPC correspond to Sections 85 and 86 of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita for later conduct. This article is general information, not advice in any case — breach prosecutions and their defences are fact-intensive, and the applicable strategy depends on the terms of the specific order.</div>
